-- allium: 3
-- llm.allium
--
-- LLM Provider: shared vocabulary and provider-agnostic contracts.
--
-- Scope: The internal request/response vocabulary every LLM provider
--        translates to and from, the exhaustive error taxonomy, and the
--        invariants that hold regardless of which provider served a turn.
--        This is the base module imported by the per-provider wire specs.
--
-- Includes: The internal ContentBlock kinds; LlmRequest / LlmResponse /
--           LlmMessage shape; the Usage token-accounting value; the
--           streaming TokenChunk; the LlmErrorKind taxonomy and its
--           automatic retry and user-resume partitions; the rule that structured quota
--           detail is carried by an error iff it is a usage-limit
--           exhaustion.
--
-- Excludes: Provider wire formats — request translation, response
--           normalization, the SSE streaming accumulators (see
--           anthropic.allium and responses.allium).
--           Error *classification* — the mapping from an HTTP status or
--           SSE payload to an LlmErrorKind. That is per-provider; this
--           module only fixes the taxonomy the classifiers must target.
--           Gateway routing, model registry, model discovery.
--
-- Self-contained: no `use` imports. The two per-provider specs import
--           this file as `llm` and create llm/LlmError and
--           llm/LlmResponse instances through their own rules.
--
-- Rationale: a 2026-05 production incident (a tool-result image fed back
--            to an OpenAI model produced HTTP 400 "Invalid value: 'text'")
--            showed the request-translation layer had no normative
--            contract. The wire discriminant a content block serializes
--            to is provider-specific; naming the internal vocabulary once
--            here lets each provider spec state its own discriminant
--            contract against a shared anchor.
--
-- Normative companions:
--   specs/llm/requirements.md  — REQ-LLM-001..009
--   specs/llm/design.md        — LlmService, LlmError, LlmErrorKind
--   specs/llm/anthropic.allium — Anthropic Messages wire contract
--   specs/llm/responses.allium — OpenAI Responses API wire contract
--
-- Cross-references:
--   crates/phoenix-ide/src/llm/types.rs — ContentBlock, LlmRequest,
--                                         LlmResponse, Usage, ImageSource
--   crates/phoenix-ide/src/llm/error.rs — LlmError, LlmErrorKind
--   crates/phoenix-ide/src/llm.rs       — TokenChunk, LlmService trait

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Role of a conversation message. Phoenix has no `system` role: system
-- prompt content is carried separately on LlmRequest.system and each
-- provider places it in its own format (Anthropic top-level `system`,
-- Responses API `instructions`).
enum MessageRole { user | assistant }

-- The discriminant of an internal ContentBlock. The string values are
-- exactly ContentBlock::type_tag() in types.rs — the serde `type` tag of
-- the internal representation, which is NOT necessarily the wire tag any
-- provider emits (that mapping is the subject of the per-provider specs).
--
-- text, image, tool_use and tool_result are the portable kinds: every
-- provider can represent them. The remaining nine are Anthropic
-- server-handled blocks — executed by the Anthropic API and preserved
-- verbatim for multi-turn history fidelity. A provider with no wire
-- equivalent must drop them through a logged capability gap, never
-- silently (see responses.allium ResponsesServerBlockDropped).
enum ContentKind {
    text
    | image
    | tool_use
    | tool_result
    | server_tool_use
    | tool_search_tool_result
    | web_search_tool_result
    | web_fetch_tool_result
    | code_execution_tool_result
    | bash_code_execution_tool_result
    | text_editor_code_execution_tool_result
    | mcp_tool_use
    | mcp_tool_result
}

-- Exhaustive error taxonomy. No `Unknown`, no catch-all: adding a class
-- forces a variant here and an explicit decision in every classifier and
-- every consumer (REQ-LLM-006). The automatic retry / user-resume split is a
-- structural property — see the derived fields on LlmError
-- and the RetryablePartition invariant.
--
-- rate_limit and usage_limit_reached are deliberately distinct: a 429
-- can be a transient per-window throttle (retry in seconds) or a quota
-- window exhausted on a calendar boundary (retry next week). Collapsing
-- them either wastes retries or misclassifies recovery.
enum LlmErrorKind {
    network
    | rate_limit
    | usage_limit_reached
    | server_error
    | server_overloaded
    | auth
    | invalid_request
    | invalid_response
    | content_filter
    | context_window_exceeded
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A base64-encoded image. The only ImageSource variant in types.rs.
value ImageBase64 {
    media_type: String
    data: String
}

-- One system-prompt segment. `cache` requests a provider cache anchor on
-- this segment (Anthropic per-block cache_control; the Responses API has
-- no per-segment cache control and ignores the flag).
value SystemContent {
    text: String
    cache: Boolean
}

-- Token accounting for one completed turn. `context_window_used` is the
-- single definition of "how full is the window" — input + output + both
-- cache buckets. Each provider's normalizer must populate the buckets so
-- this sum equals the provider's own reported total (see
-- ResponsesCachedSubsetOfInput in responses.allium).
value Usage {
    input_tokens: Integer
    output_tokens: Integer
    cache_creation_tokens: Integer
    cache_read_tokens: Integer

    context_window_used:
        input_tokens + output_tokens + cache_creation_tokens + cache_read_tokens
}

-- One content block inside a message. Phoenix's internal representation,
-- before any provider translation. `kind` is the discriminator.
--
-- `tool_result_images` is the load-bearing field for the wire
-- discriminant contract: a tool_result with a non-empty image list
-- forces a structured (multi-part) wire encoding, and that is exactly
-- the path the 2026-05 OpenAI 400 incident exercised. The list is empty
-- for every kind other than tool_result.
value ContentBlock {
    kind: ContentKind
    tool_result_images: List<ImageBase64>
    tool_result_is_error: Boolean
}

-- One conversation message handed to a provider.
value LlmMessage {
    role: MessageRole
    content: List<ContentBlock>
}

-- A provider-agnostic completion request. `tool_count` abstracts the
-- ToolDefinition list (the spec cares whether tools are present, not
-- their schemas). `cache_key` is the caller-chosen prompt-cache cohort
-- id (PromptCacheKey) — mandatory on the typed request; consumed by the
-- Responses API as `prompt_cache_key`, ignored by Anthropic.
value LlmRequest {
    system: List<SystemContent>
    messages: List<LlmMessage>
    tool_count: Integer
    max_tokens: Integer?
    cache_key: String
}

-- A streaming chunk forwarded to the UI mid-turn. Phoenix's TokenChunk
-- has exactly one variant: a text delta. Tool-call argument fragments
-- are NOT streamed — they are accumulated provider-side and only appear
-- in the final assembled LlmResponse. See the per-provider streaming
-- accumulators for where text chunks are forwarded.
value TokenChunk {
    text: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A provider-agnostic completion response. Created by each provider's
-- normalizer. `end_turn` means the model has nothing further to do (no
-- pending tool calls). The normalized content blocks are not modelled as
-- a field — per-block normalization is documented in each provider
-- spec's @guidance; this entity exists so cross-provider invariants
-- (token accounting) can range over completed responses.
entity LlmResponse {
    end_turn: Boolean
    usage: Usage
}

-- A classified LLM failure. Created by the per-provider error
-- classifiers (Anthropic SSE / HTTP status; Responses SSE / codex
-- backend). `kind` is set once at creation and never transitions.
--
-- `auto_retryable` and `user_resumable` are derived, not stored: each
-- decision is a pure function of `kind`, so they cannot drift out of sync
-- with the taxonomy or with each other.
-- `has_quota` records whether structured QuotaDetails is attached; it is
-- bound to `usage_limit_reached` by QuotaBoundToUsageLimit.
entity LlmError {
    kind: LlmErrorKind
    message: String
    -- True when a recovery mechanism (e.g. the credential helper) is
    -- already running and may resolve this error; the state machine
    -- waits rather than treating the error as terminal.
    recovery_in_progress: Boolean
    -- True iff structured codex QuotaDetails (plan, reset time, window
    -- snapshots, credits) is attached to this error.
    has_quota: Boolean

    -- Automatic runtime retry decision. A malformed response is a
    -- server/transport fault, so it joins the transient-failure kinds; every
    -- other kind requires stopping the in-flight turn.
    auto_retryable: kind = network or kind = rate_limit or kind = server_error or kind = invalid_response

    -- User-triggered resume from a persisted conversation error. Auth is not
    -- auto-retryable, but is resumable after credentials are fixed.
    -- usage_limit_reached is never auto-retried, but its quota window resets
    -- on a clock boundary, so the user can resume once the window clears.
    user_resumable: kind = auth or kind = network or kind = rate_limit or kind = server_error or kind = invalid_response or kind = server_overloaded or kind = usage_limit_reached
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Structured quota detail is attached to an error if and only if the
-- error is a usage-limit exhaustion. The `usage_limit_reached`
-- constructor in error.rs always builds QuotaDetails; no other
-- constructor sets it. A violation would mean either a quota indicator
-- with no backing data, or an exhaustion error the UI cannot render
-- plan-aware recovery for.
invariant QuotaBoundToUsageLimit {
    for e in LlmErrors:
        e.has_quota implies e.kind = usage_limit_reached
        (e.kind = usage_limit_reached) implies e.has_quota
}

-- The auto-retryable set is exactly {network, rate_limit, server_error,
-- invalid_response}.
-- Because `auto_retryable` is a derived field this holds by construction; the
-- invariant is the place a future taxonomy change is forced to restate
-- the partition deliberately rather than let a new kind default into
-- either bucket.
invariant AutoRetryablePartition {
    for e in LlmErrors:
        e.auto_retryable = (e.kind = network or e.kind = rate_limit or e.kind = server_error or e.kind = invalid_response)
}

invariant UserResumablePartition {
    for e in LlmErrors:
        e.user_resumable = (e.kind = auth or e.kind = network or e.kind = rate_limit or e.kind = server_error or e.kind = invalid_response or e.kind = server_overloaded or e.kind = usage_limit_reached)
}

-- Token accounting is non-negative. Each count is an unsigned quantity
-- from the provider; the normalizers must not produce a negative bucket
-- (the Responses normalizer subtracts cached tokens out of input_tokens
-- and must saturate at zero — see responses.allium).
invariant UsageNonNegative {
    for r in LlmResponses:
        r.usage.input_tokens >= 0
        r.usage.output_tokens >= 0
        r.usage.cache_creation_tokens >= 0
        r.usage.cache_read_tokens >= 0
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------
-- Both resolved 2026-05-22. Kept as prose so the file stays parseable.
--
-- Q1. RESOLVED — accept as shipped behaviour. TokenChunk is text-only.
--     design.md describes a richer four-variant TokenChunk
--     (ToolUseStart / ToolUseInput / ToolUseDone) for streaming
--     tool-call construction to the UI, but the shipped enum in llm.rs
--     has only Text; tool arguments are accumulated provider-side and
--     never streamed. This spec models the shipped behaviour. If
--     streamed tool-call construction is built, this value type and the
--     per-provider streaming rules grow together.
--
-- Q2. RESOLVED — keep as-is. content_filter and context_window_exceeded
--     are in the taxonomy and reachable from the Responses classifier
--     (classify_responses_error) but the Anthropic provider never
--     produces them — its SSE error mapping has no branch for either, so
--     an Anthropic context-window overflow surfaces as the generic
--     invalid_request kind. Accepted: invalid_request and
--     context_window_exceeded remains non-auto-retryable and non-user-resumable.
--     behaviour is identical, and nothing currently consumes the finer
--     kinds (both are #[allow(dead_code)] in error.rs). Revisit if a
--     consumer needs the finer Anthropic classification.
